    "turn_text": "lot of, you have to manage people, you have to have a budget, you have to have an objective. So small business owners, and there are many of them in the Congress, do have a lot of skill sets, you have to market yourself around the community. And small business owners know the tap of the town. So every in-district period, we're in a nice two-week period right now, so we're going all over Central Florida. We do a small business tour in different areas of the district to meet with folks and so you really get a sense of oh the traffic here is tough or oh we could use a fix and an environmental issue here or oh we need more economic development dollars coming in to help. make this community more vibrant or this is another issue popping up. So they do work on a lot of issues that because these civic issues affect their business, they're knowledgeable of them and have opinions about them, of course, right? And then the kind of passionate takes from a small business. Look, if you don't get up every day and do the work that maybe not everybody else is doing because you're the boss and it falls to you, your business doesn't thrive, right? And you have to connect with people. So that also is very common with local government and state government and going on to Congress. So there's a lot of skill sets that translate, but also as you're balancing out whether it's in a crisis or when you're dealing with regulatory issues or tax issues, you see you know, yes, we have to do protections to help people. But if you go too far, then it could be burdensome. So that balance, you're never done with that. It's a constant continuum. The biggest example I could give you is when we were in the height of the pandemic. Yeah. And. We put forward the paycheck protection program which had a huge bipartisan support because most of our small businesses across the nation They don't have like three four months of payroll just lined up there They're almost hand-to-mouth. Maybe they have like one month",
