    "turn_text": "in what they're doing by the power of the people supporting it. But then it's always in the details, right? So I think that where we could start is bipartisan privacy legislation. We passed out of the committee last year unanimously at our committee. The Senate didn't think it went far enough. Folks from California had their own version that they were worried about preemption. Now a couple other states who are doing their own are worried about preemption. But nothing is more interstate commerce than the internal. It virtually has no respect for any barrier across the world. And that's one of the beauties is you can communicate with someone from Australia or from South Africa or from Brazil. And it's streamless other than the time zones. So we definitely need the federal government to be more involved in this, but just with basic rules of the road. And so, yeah, like with some other major civil rights and regulatory reforms, it may take a while to get it to what we all can agree on, but I strongly think we could at least protect privacy and empower parents and get that through this term. So my hope is that that'll happen.",
