    "turn_text": "go for a walk out or whatever you do to relax, find yours then and get back to it. Those would be the fundamental ways and then relationships matter. Over time, you develop these relationships that you get 10 years, 15 years, like one of your professors, Dennis Ross. I served in the Congress and in the State House with me. Since 2007, I've known Dennis. So even as we may get on the floor and be passionate about an issue and be on the opposite side of one, neither of us are going to take it personal. And you don't personalize it. If you are really passionate about an issue, you talk about the issue and how it's affecting your constituents. You don't call someone names or say it's their fault. They're coming from another position and you have to have respect for that. But I think that's the biggest thing. Respect is the biggest, but a lot of these other lessons can help too.",
